    "local": {
      "type": "local-area",
      "blocks": {
        "b1": {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "What Highland calls us for",
            "text": "<p>Highland sits on the bench between American Fork and Alpine, and the lots are generous. Homes are set back off the road, which means long approach driveways, and there's usually room beside the house for an RV or a toy hauler. That's most of what we pour up there: <a href=\"/pages/concrete-driveways\">driveways</a> and <a href=\"/pages/rv-pads\">RV pads</a>, with patios close behind.</p>"
          }
        },
        "b2": {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "More replacement than new",
            "text": "<p>A lot of Highland's neighborhoods went in through the 1990s and early 2000s, and the original flatwork is now at the end of its life — spalling at the surface, cracks that have opened past the point of patching, sections lifted by tree roots. Replacing it properly means taking the old slab out, fixing whatever the base was doing wrong underneath it, and pouring back to a spec that lasts. We do all three, and the old concrete leaves on our truck.</p>"
          }
        },
        "b3": {
          "type": "detail",
          "settings": {
            "title": "A driveway that carries the load",
            "text": "Four inches over compacted base is fine for cars. Where a truck, a trailer or an RV turns and parks, we go thicker and add steel — that's specified at the estimate, not guessed at on the day."
          }
        },
        "b4": {
          "type": "detail",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Tear-out and haul-off included",
            "text": "No separate demo contractor, no dumpster parked in front of your house for two weeks. The old slab comes out and goes away as part of the same job."
          }
        },
        "b5": {
          "type": "detail",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Joints cut where cracks want to go",
            "text": "Concrete cracks; the craft is deciding where. Control joints get placed and cut on time so the slab breaks along a straight line you can't see instead of across the middle."
          }
        }
      },
